use std
into bc.amni

Namespace bc; imports math. Block-compressed texture formats BC1, BC3, BC4, BC5 and BC7 on the CPU: decoders that give the same texels a GPU gives, and encoders. A format is named by an int kind: constants `BC1 = 1`, `BC3 = 3`, `BC4 = 4`, `BC5 = 5`, `BC7 = 7`. Every format stores a picture as 4 by 4 texel blocks in row order (blocks across = (w + 3) / 4, blocks down = (h + 3) / 4); a block is 8 bytes for BC1 and BC4 and 16 bytes for the others. Inside a block texel i = x + 4 y. Multi-byte fields are little-endian. All pictures on the CPU side are RGBA8, 4 bytes per pixel, top row first. Everything is integer math unless a block says otherwise; no allocation per block (work in fixed arrays), because whole 1024 by 1024 pictures go through these functions. The module starts with exactly these tables:

```he
let P2 int[64] = [52428, 34952, 61166, 60616, 51328, 65260, 65224, 60544, 51200, 65516, 65152, 59392, 65512, 65280, 65520, 61440, 63248, 142, 28928, 2254, 140, 29456, 12544, 36046, 2188, 12560, 26214, 13932, 6120, 4080, 29070, 14748, 43690, 61680, 23130, 13260, 15420, 21930, 38550, 42330, 29646, 5064, 12876, 15324, 27030, 49980, 39270, 1632, 626, 1252, 20032, 10016, 51510, 37740, 14790, 25500, 37686, 40134, 33150, 59160, 52464, 4044, 30532, 60962];
let P3 int[64] = [2858963024, 1784303680, 1515864576, 1414570152, 2779054080, 2694860880, 1431675040, 1515868240, 2857697280, 2857719040, 2863289600, 2425393296, 2492765332, 2762253476, 2846200912, 705315408, 2777960512, 172118100, 2779096320, 1436590240, 2829603924, 1785348160, 2762231808, 437912832, 5285028, 2862977168, 342452500, 1768494080, 2693105056, 2860651540, 1352967248, 1784283648, 2846195712, 1351655592, 2829094992, 606348324, 11162880, 613566756, 608801316, 1352993360, 1342874960, 2863285316, 1717960704, 2778768800, 1352683680, 1764256040, 1152035396, 1717986816, 2856600644, 1420317864, 2508232064, 2526451200, 2824098984, 2157286784, 2853442580, 2526412800, 2863272980, 2689618080, 2695210400, 2516582400, 1082146944, 2846402984, 2863311428, 709513812];
let A2 int[64] = [15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 2, 8, 2, 2, 8, 8, 15, 2, 8, 2, 2, 8, 8, 2, 2, 15, 15, 6, 8, 2, 8, 15, 15, 2, 8, 2, 2, 2, 15, 15, 6, 6, 2, 6, 8, 15, 15, 2, 2, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 2, 2, 15];
let A3A int[64] = [3, 3, 15, 15, 8, 3, 15, 15, 8, 8, 6, 6, 6, 5, 3, 3, 3, 3, 8, 15, 3, 3, 6, 10, 5, 8, 8, 6, 8, 5, 15, 15, 8, 15, 3, 5, 6, 10, 8, 15, 15, 3, 15, 5, 15, 15, 15, 15, 3, 15, 5, 5, 5, 8, 5, 10, 5, 10, 8, 13, 15, 12, 3, 3];
let A3B int[64] = [15, 8, 8, 3, 15, 15, 3, 8, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 8, 15, 8, 15, 3, 15, 8, 15, 8, 3, 15, 6, 10, 15, 15, 10, 8, 15, 3, 15, 10, 10, 8, 9, 10, 6, 15, 8, 15, 3, 6, 6, 8, 15, 3, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 3, 15, 15, 8];
let W2 int[4] = [0, 21, 43, 64];
let W3 int[8] = [0, 9, 18, 27, 37, 46, 55, 64];
let W4 int[16] = [0, 4, 9, 13, 17, 21, 26, 30, 34, 38, 43, 47, 51, 55, 60, 64];
let M_SUBSETS int[8] = [3, 2, 3, 2, 1, 1, 1, 2];
let M_PART int[8] = [4, 6, 6, 6, 0, 0, 0, 6];
let M_CBITS int[8] = [4, 6, 5, 7, 5, 7, 7, 5];
let M_ABITS int[8] = [0, 0, 0, 0, 6, 8, 7, 5];
let M_PBITS int[8] = [1, 2, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1];
let M_IBITS int[8] = [3, 3, 2, 2, 2, 2, 4, 2];
```

`block_bytes(kind int) int` is 8 for BC1 and BC4, 16 for BC3, BC5 and BC7, and 0 for any other kind.

`size(kind int, w int, h int) int` is the byte count of one picture: `block_bytes(kind) * ((w + 3) / 4) * ((h + 3) / 4)`.

`color_block(b, at int, four bool, px, o int)` (b: read-only byte slice; px: byte slice it writes into) decodes the 8-byte BC1 color block at `b[at]` into 16 RGBA texels written at `px[o + 4 i]`. c0 and c1 are 16-bit RGB565 values (bytes 0-1 and 2-3; red in the top 5 bits); expand a 5-bit value v as `(v << 3) | (v >> 2)` and a 6-bit one as `(v << 2) | (v >> 4)`. When `four` is true or c0 > c1 (compared as 16-bit numbers) the palette is e0, e1, `(2 e0 + e1 + 1) / 3`, `(e0 + 2 e1 + 1) / 3` per channel, all alpha 255; otherwise it is e0, e1, `(e0 + e1 + 1) / 2`, and a fourth entry of 0, 0, 0 with alpha 0. Bytes 4-7 hold 2 bits per texel, texel 0 in the lowest bits of byte 4.

`alpha_block(b, at int, px, o int, step int)` (b: read-only byte slice; px: byte slice it writes into) decodes the 8-byte BC4 block at `b[at]` into 16 single values written at `px[o + step * i]`. a0 = byte 0, a1 = byte 1. The 8-entry palette comes from `alpha_palette(a0 int, a1 int, pal)` (pal: int slice of 8 it fills), a separate function this block defines: entries 0 and 1 are a0 and a1. Each in-between entry sits halfway between the exact fraction and the 6-bit weights some GPUs use, rounded to nearest, so it is within 1 of either: when a0 > a1, for k = 1..6 with W = 9, 18, 27, 37, 46, 55, entry k + 1 is `((((7 - k) a0 + k a1) * 64 + (a0 (64 - W) + a1 W) * 7 + 448) / 896`; otherwise for k = 1..4 with W = 13, 26, 38, 51, entry k + 1 is `((((5 - k) a0 + k a1) * 64 + (a0 (64 - W) + a1 W) * 5 + 320) / 640`, then entry 6 is 0 and entry 7 is 255. Bytes 2-7 are a 48-bit little-endian number holding 3 bits per texel, texel 0 lowest.

`bits(b, at int, pos int, n int) int` (b: read-only byte slice) reads n bits (n from 0 to 8) of the 16-byte block at `b[at]` starting at bit `pos`, where bit p is bit `p & 7` of byte `p >> 3`, the first bit read being the lowest bit of the result. n = 0 gives 0.

`bc7_block(b, at int, px, o int)` (b: read-only byte slice; px: byte slice it writes into) decodes one BC7 block into 16 RGBA texels at `px[o + 4 i]`, exactly as the format defines it:
- The mode m is the index of the lowest set bit of byte 0; when byte 0 is 0 all 64 output bytes are 0 and that is all. Reading continues at bit m + 1.
- Read in this order: the partition number (`M_PART[m]` bits); for mode 4 a 2-bit rotation then a 1-bit index selector, for mode 5 a 2-bit rotation; then the endpoints; then the p-bits; then the indices.
- Subsets ns = `M_SUBSETS[m]`; there are 2 ns endpoints, numbered subset s endpoint e as 2 s + e. Endpoint fields come channel by channel: all red values (endpoint 0, 1, 2, ...), then all green, then all blue, each `M_CBITS[m]` bits; then, only when `M_ABITS[m]` > 0, all alpha values with `M_ABITS[m]` bits each.
- P-bits: modes 0, 3, 6, 7 have one p-bit per endpoint (2 ns bits, in endpoint order); mode 1 has one per subset (2 bits; subset s's bit applies to both of its endpoints); modes 2, 4, 5 have none. A p-bit is appended below every channel the endpoint has (red, green, blue, and alpha when the mode has alpha): `v = (v << 1) | p`, making the channel one bit wider.
- Expand each channel from its width n (after the p-bit) to 8 bits: `(v << (8 - n)) | (v >> (2 n - 8))`. In modes without alpha, alpha is 255.
- The subset of texel i: 0 when ns is 1; bit i of `P2[partition]` when ns is 2; `(P3[partition] >> (2 i)) & 3` when ns is 3.
- Anchor texels store one bit fewer (their top index bit is 0 and not stored): texel 0 always; with 2 subsets also texel `A2[partition]`; with 3 subsets also texels `A3A[partition]` and `A3B[partition]`.
- Indices: modes 0-3, 6, 7 have one index per texel of `M_IBITS[m]` bits, read texel 0 to 15, used for all four channels. Mode 4 has 16 two-bit indices (31 bits, texel 0 is the anchor) followed by 16 three-bit indices (47 bits, texel 0 is the anchor); when the selector is 0 the color channels use the two-bit set and alpha the three-bit set, when it is 1 color uses the three-bit set and alpha the two-bit set. Mode 5 has 16 two-bit color indices (31 bits) followed by 16 two-bit alpha indices (31 bits).
- A channel's value is `((64 - wt) * e0 + wt * e1 + 32) >> 6` with e0 and e1 the texel's subset's two endpoints and wt from W2, W3 or W4 by the index's bit count.
- Last, rotation (modes 4 and 5): 1 swaps alpha with red, 2 swaps alpha with green, 3 swaps alpha with blue.

`decode(kind int, blocks, w int, h int, out) bool` (blocks: read-only byte slice; out: growable byte array by reference) clears `out`, resizes it to `w * h * 4` and fills it with the decoded picture; false (and `out` left empty) when kind is unknown, w or h is not positive, or `blocks` is shorter than `size(kind, w, h)`. Each block is decoded into a local 64-byte array and only the texels inside the picture are copied (w and h need not be multiples of 4). BC1: `color_block` with four = false. BC3: the first 8 bytes are an alpha block (`alpha_block` into the alpha bytes), the next 8 a color block with four = true whose alpha is then replaced by the alpha block's values. BC4: one alpha block giving red; green and blue 0, alpha 255. BC5: two alpha blocks giving red then green; blue 0, alpha 255. BC7: `bc7_block`.

`fetch(rgba, w int, h int, bx int, by int, px)` (rgba: read-only byte slice; px: byte slice it writes into) copies the 4 by 4 texels of block (bx, by) into `px` (64 bytes), repeating the last column and row for texels past the picture's edge.

`pack_color(px, b, at int, four bool)` (px: read-only byte slice; b: byte slice it writes into) writes an 8-byte BC1 color block for the 16 texels in `px` (alpha ignored) at `b[at]`, in float math: the mean color and the 3 by 3 covariance of the 16 colors; the main axis by 8 rounds of power iteration started from the covariance's diagonal-largest axis (start vector = (1, 1, 1) projected through the covariance once; if the axis's length is under 1e-6 use (1, 1, 1)); project each color on the axis and take the smallest and largest projections as the two end colors (mean + axis * projection, clamped to 0..255); round each end to RGB565 (5 bits: `(v * 31 + 127) / 255` on the rounded 8-bit value; 6 bits: `(v * 63 + 127) / 255`). Then two rounds of refinement: assign each texel the nearest of the four palette colors (the palette exactly as `color_block` builds it with four colors), then solve the least-squares problem for the two ends given those assignments (weights 0, 1, 1/3, 2/3 toward the second end for palette entries 0, 1, 2, 3; per channel, the usual 2 by 2 normal equations; skip the update when the determinant is under 1e-9), re-round to RGB565, and keep the new ends only when the block's total squared error got smaller. Make c0 the larger 16-bit value and c1 the smaller (swap them and re-derive the indices when needed). When c0 equals c1 write all indices 0. Final indices are the nearest palette entry for each texel using the decoder's own palette for the final (c0, c1); write c0, c1 little-endian and the 2-bit indices, texel 0 in the lowest bits.

`pack_alpha(px, o int, step int, b, at int)` (px: read-only byte slice; b: byte slice it writes into) writes an 8-byte BC4 block at `b[at]` for the 16 values `px[o + step * i]`: a0 = the largest value, a1 = the smallest. When they are equal write a0, a1 and all indices 0. Otherwise (a0 > a1) fill the palette by calling `alpha_palette(a0, a1, pal)`, the function the `alpha_block` block defines (no palette math here), and each texel takes the palette entry nearest its value; write a0, a1, then the 48 bits of indices, texel 0 lowest.

`pack_bc7(px, b, at int)` (px: read-only byte slice; b: byte slice it writes into) writes a 16-byte BC7 block in mode 6 (one subset, 7-bit red, green, blue and alpha endpoints, one p-bit per endpoint, 4-bit indices) for the 16 RGBA texels in `px`, in float math over the four channels:
- The mean and the 4 by 4 covariance; the main axis by 8 rounds of power iteration (start: the covariance applied to (1, 1, 1, 1); if the result is shorter than 1e-6 the axis is (1, 1, 1, 1) normalized); the two ends are mean + axis * (smallest and largest projection), clamped to 0..255.
- `quant(end)`: for p = 0 and p = 1 each channel becomes `q = clamp(round((x - p) / 2), 0, 127)` with value `2 q + p`; keep the p with the smaller summed squared error over the four channels. One exception: when all 16 texels have alpha 255, both ends always use p = 1 and an alpha field of 127, so alpha decodes to exactly 255. That gives 8-bit end values and the 7-bit fields plus p-bit.
- Assign indices: for each texel the index k in 0..15 whose palette color `((64 - W4[k]) * e0 + W4[k] * e1 + 32) >> 6` (integer, per channel, on the quantized ends) is nearest in summed squared error over four channels. To keep it fast, first estimate k from the texel's projection onto the line between the ends (`t * 15` rounded) and test only k - 1, k and k + 1.
- Two refinement rounds: with wt = W4[k] / 64 per texel, solve per channel the 2 by 2 least-squares problem for the two ends (skip when the determinant is under 1e-9), clamp, `quant` again, reassign indices, and keep the result only when the block's total squared error got smaller.
- The anchor rule: texel 0's index must be below 8. When it is not, swap the two ends (fields and p-bits) and replace every index k by 15 - k.
- Write: bit 6 set alone in the mode field (byte 0's low 7 bits are 1000000 in binary, that is the value 64), then from bit 7: red e0, red e1, green e0, green e1, blue e0, blue e1, alpha e0, alpha e1 (7 bits each), p-bit of e0, p-bit of e1, then texel 0's index in 3 bits and texels 1..15 in 4 bits each. Build the 128 bits with a small local helper that appends n bits, lowest first, into the 16 bytes (zeroed first). This block defines `pack_bc7` and helpers of its own only; it does not define `pack_alpha` or any function named elsewhere in this file.

`encode(kind int, rgba, w int, h int, out) bool` (rgba: read-only byte slice; out: growable byte array by reference) clears `out`, resizes it to `size(kind, w, h)` and writes one block per 4 by 4 group using `fetch`; false (out left empty) when kind is unknown, w or h is not positive or `rgba` is shorter than `w * h * 4`. BC1: `pack_color` with four = false (alpha is ignored; every texel comes out opaque). BC3: `pack_alpha` on the alpha bytes, then `pack_color` with four = true. BC4: `pack_alpha` on red. BC5: `pack_alpha` on red, then on green. BC7: `pack_bc7`. This block defines `encode` alone: it calls the `pack_alpha`, `pack_color` and `pack_bc7` defined earlier and defines no packing function of its own.

`halve(rgba, w int, h int, srgb bool, out)` returning two ints (rgba: read-only byte slice; out: growable byte array by reference) makes the next smaller mip: the result is `max(w / 2, 1)` by `max(h / 2, 1)`, written into `out` (cleared first), and those two sizes are returned. Each result pixel is the average of the 2 by 2 source pixels it covers (source coordinates clamped to the edge when a side is 1). When `srgb` is true red, green and blue are averaged in linear light: convert each byte with the sRGB decoding function (c / 255 <= 0.04045 gives c / 12.92, else ((c + 0.055) / 1.055)^2.4) through a 256-entry float table built once into a module `var`, average, convert back with the sRGB encoding function (x <= 0.0031308 gives 12.92 x, else 1.055 x^(1/2.4) - 0.055) and round to the nearest byte; alpha is always a plain average rounded to nearest. When `srgb` is false all four channels are plain averages rounded to nearest (`(sum + 2) / 4`).
